    cmp: { buy_hold: 'Always 100% SPY', static60: 'Static 60% equities', rule: '<b>This site’s rule (current)</b>', rule_v1: 'Rule v1 (previous)', rule_v3: 'Rule v3 (from 2026-10-01)', rule_no_shock: 'This rule without tightening shock', trend_only: 'Trend only (10-month average)', macro_only: 'Recession probability only' },
    robust: (n, c, d, sh) => `Robustness: across ${n} threshold settings (stance cut-offs and tightening-shock definitions), CAGR ${c}, max drawdown ${d}, Sharpe ${sh}. Results do not hinge on one parameter choice.`,
    trkCrashH: 'Behaviour in four major drawdowns (month-end prices)',
    colPeriod: 'Period', colSpyDD: 'SPY drawdown', colRuleDD: 'Rule drawdown', colStart: 'Stance at start', colDefMonths: 'Defensive / balanced months',
    trkRateH: 'How do stocks do in hiking cycles? (S&P 500 price index)',
    regime: { all: 'All months', hiking: 'Hiking (≥ 1 hike in last 6 months)', hiking_hot: 'Hiking and CPI > 3%', hiking_cool: 'Hiking and CPI ≤ 3%', not_hiking: 'Not hiking' },
    trkRateNote: 'Since 1955, returns after hiking months are only slightly worse than otherwise — far from “hikes mean a crash”; the Fed usually hikes into a strong economy. The real danger is runaway inflation forcing fast hikes (as in 2022), which is what the tightening-shock signal targets. The “years” count is the true number of independent samples, far fewer than months.',
    trkLimitsH: 'Limitations you should know',
    trkLimits: `<li><b>Hindsight in indicator choice</b>: the yield curve and the Sahm rule are famous precisely because they worked in the past. Future performance will likely be worse than the backtest.</li>
      <li><b>Small sample</b>: only 4 recessions and a handful of hiking cycles since 1985. The “years” column is the true independent sample size.</li>
      <li><b>The value is in risk-adjusted return</b>: long-run CAGR close to buy-and-hold, max drawdown roughly halved, clearly higher Sharpe and Calmar.</li>
      <li><b>The trend does most of the work</b>: a 10-month average alone captures most of the effect; the macro signals add earlier turns (2007), protection in inflationary bear markets (2022) and a faster return to Offensive (v3) — see the comparison table above.</li>`,

