    fourierH: '③ Fourier: are there really “sine waves” in the economy?',
    fourierLede: 'Compute the periodogram and compare it with <b>red noise</b> of the same autocorrelation (AR(1), 1000 Monte Carlo runs). Only peaks above the noise 95% line count as real cycles — otherwise it may be the Slutsky (1927) effect: summed random shocks also look cyclical.',
    specLabel: { ip: 'Industrial production, quarterly annualized growth', unrate: 'Unemployment rate' },
    years: 'years', periodYears: 'Period (years)', power: 'Power',
    specObs: 'Data', spec95: 'Red noise 95%', specMed: 'Red noise median',
    periodTip: (p) => `Period ${p} years`,
    specSig: (list) => `Significant periods found: ${list} years.`,
    specNone: 'Verdict: no frequency rises significantly above red noise. Economic fluctuations are recurrent, not periodic — you cannot extrapolate dates like a sine wave.',
    specRes: (y, list) => `Resolution limit: ${y} years of data can only resolve the longest periods ${list} years — the 75–100-year “long debt cycle” appears about once in the sample, so Fourier analysis cannot test it.`,

    bandsH: '④ Band decomposition: the article’s formula, done properly with a band-pass filter',
    bandsLede: 'log(industrial production) = trend (&gt;30y, the “DC baseline”) + medium 8–30y (financial/credit cycle) + business cycle 1.5–8y + noise (&lt;1.5y). Christiano–Fitzgerald band-pass filter; unit: % deviation from trend. Note: band-pass output <b>always</b> looks wavy — that is the filter’s shape and does not prove a cycle exists (Harvey &amp; Jaeger, 1993). Section ③ is the actual test.',
    band: {
      trend: 'Trend >30y (DC baseline, log level)', medium: 'Medium 8–30y (financial/credit cycle, %)',
      business: 'Business cycle 1.5–8y (short debt cycle, %)', noise: 'Noise <1.5y (%)',
    },
    realtime: 'Real-time estimate (what you could see then)',
    relNote: (n, s, sl, c, now, slope) => `<b>End-point problem</b>: the blue line uses all data (including the “future”); the dashed orange line is the real-time value computed with only the data available at each date.
      Across ${n} historical dates, real-time and final values agree in sign ${s} of the time and in direction ${sl}; correlation ${c}.
      So “where are we on the wave now” is wrong about 1 time in 4 — which is why we use Bayesian probabilities instead of certainty.
      Current business-cycle component ${now}% (3-month change ${slope}pp; end-point value, indicative only). Gray = NBER recessions.`,

    longH: '⑤ The observable part of the long debt cycle',
    longLede: 'Dalio’s long debt cycle cannot be estimated spectrally (less than one full cycle in the sample), but its <b>state variables</b> can be monitored: private credit/GDP and its gap (BIS method, one-sided HP filter λ=400000; a gap &gt; 10pp is the historical pre-crisis warning zone), and federal debt/GDP.',
    debtGdp: 'Debt / GDP (%)', privCredit: 'Private non-financial credit', fedDebt: 'Federal debt',
